  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: \nThe `BuggyExpandable` component is fundamentally inaccessible. By rendering an interactive disclosure trigger as an unstyled `<div>` without native button semantics, keyboard listeners, or `aria-expanded` state, it completely blocks keyboard-only users and silently fails screen reader users. The component requires a complete architectural refactor to native semantic HTML before it can be considered for production.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\n*Based on the component type (collapsible/disclosure widget):*\n- **Prediction 1**: Non-semantic `div`/`span` trigger lacking keyboard accessibility (`Enter`/`Space`) and tab sequence inclusion. *(Confirmed: `<div>` with `onClick` only, inaccessible via Tab or keyboard activation).*\n- **Prediction 2**: Missing `aria-expanded` state communication. *(Confirmed: completely absent).*\n- **Prediction 3**: Missing programmatic relationship between trigger and controlled region via `aria-controls` / `id`. *(Confirmed: completely absent).*\n- **Prediction 4**: Visual state glyphs (`+` / `\u2212`) announced literally by screen readers. *(Confirmed: literal string rendered in DOM without `aria-hidden=\"true\"`).*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n#### 1. Total Keyboard Inoperability via Non-Interactive Element Trigger\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-18`\n  ```jsx\n  <div\n    className=\"expandable-trigger\"\n    onClick={toggle}\n  >\n  ```\n- **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users, switch device users, screen reader users navigating by tab stop.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: \n  - [WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/#keyboard)\n  - [WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/#name-role-value)\n  - [WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure (Show/Hide) Pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/disclosure/)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Plain `<div>` elements are not in the browser's default tab order (`tabIndex` is absent) and do not respond to `Enter` or `Space` key presses. A keyboard-only user navigating the page cannot tab to this element, inspect it, or expand the section. The functionality is 100% blocked. ARIA first rule violation: native `<button>` must be used.\n- **Fix**: Replace `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">` with a native `<button type=\"button\" className=\"expandable-trigger\">`. Native buttons automatically supply focusability, keyboard event handling (`Space`/`Enter`), and the correct role in the accessibility tree.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: disclosure-trigger-nonsemantic-div\nfingerprint: a1b2c3d4e5f60718\nsource: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-18\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), APG Disclosure\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 2.1.1 Keyboard, 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen_reader=HIGH, low_vision=MEDIUM, cognitive=LOW\nevidence: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12: <div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>\nreproduction_steps: 1. Render BuggyExpandable. 2. Attempt to navigate to the trigger using Tab key. 3. Attempt activation via Space or Enter.\nexpected_behavior: Control is focusable via Tab, has accessible role \"button\", and toggles on Space/Enter activation.\nactual_behavior: Control is skipped in tab order; keyboard users cannot reach or activate the disclosure.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Missing Role and Dynamic State Communication (`aria-expanded`)\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-18`\n  ```jsx\n  <div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>\n    {title}\n    {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n  </div>\n  ```\n- **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS).\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: \n  - [WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/#name-role-value)\n  - [WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure (Show/Hide) Pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/disclosure/)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Even if focused or clicked via a mouse or virtual cursor, screen readers announce this element as generic text. It lacks the `button` role and omits `aria-expanded=\"true|false\"`. Assistive technology users receive no indication that the item is interactive or whether the controlled panel is currently open or collapsed.\n- **Fix**: When migrating to `<button>`, add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the button trigger.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: disclosure-missing-aria-expanded\nfingerprint: b2c3d4e5f6071829\nsource: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-18\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, APG Disclosure Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-18: Missing aria-expanded on toggle trigger\nreproduction_steps: 1. Inspect element in accessibility inspector or navigate with screen reader. 2. Observe role and state.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader announces \"[Title], button, collapsed\" or \"expanded\".\nactual_behavior: Screen reader announces \"[Title] plus\" as plain text with no state or role.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n#### 1. Missing Programmatic Relationship Between Trigger and Panel (`aria-controls`)\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-24`\n  ```jsx\n  {isExpanded && (\n    <div className=\"expandable-content\">\n      {content}\n    </div>\n  )}\n  ```\n- **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: \n  - [WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/#info-and-relationships)\n  - [WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/disclosure/)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Per the WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure specification, the disclosure trigger button should programmatically identify the region it manages via `aria-controls=\"<content-id>\"`, and the controlled container must carry matching `id=\"<content-id>\"`. Without this relationship, users of assistive tech cannot determine what area of the DOM is being modified.\n- **Fix**: Generate a unique ID (e.g., using `useId()` in React) and assign `aria-controls={contentId}` to the trigger button, setting `id={contentId}` on the content container.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n#### 1. Redundant Visual Glyph Exposed to Speech Synthesis\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:17`\n  ```jsx\n  {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n  ```\n- **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: \n  - [WCAG 1.1.1 Non-text Content (Level A)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/#non-text-content)\n  - [WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/#name-role-value)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Mathematical operator characters `+` and `\u2212` (or `-`) are read out phonetically by screen readers as \"plus\" and \"minus\". When `aria-expanded` is properly implemented, the user hears redundant and confusing speech: *\"Frequently Asked Questions, button, collapsed, plus\"*.\n- **Fix**: Wrap visual state indicators in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">`:\n  ```jsx\n  <span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (best practices)\n\n1. **Semantic Heading Wrapper**: In most UI designs, expandable sections serve as accordion items or structured content subsections. Wrap the trigger button in a semantic heading (e.g. `<h3><button ...>{title}...</button></h3>`) so screen reader users can navigate directly between sections using heading navigation keys (`H` / `1-6`). (WCAG 1.3.1).\n2. **Region Landmark on Panel**: If the expandable content constitutes a significant standalone section, mark the content container with `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby={triggerId}` to allow fast container navigation.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases)\n\n- **Missing Native Semantics**: No `<button>` element used for interactive control.\n- **Missing State Attribute**: No `aria-expanded` attribute.\n- **Missing Element Association**: No `aria-controls` referencing an `id` on `.expandable-content`.\n- **Missing Focus Indicator Contract**: Because a custom `div` was used, native focus ring styles are absent. Custom focus outlines (`:focus-visible`) meeting 3:1 contrast against background (WCAG 2.4.7 / 2.4.13) must be added to the button.\n- **Missing Unique ID Association**: No unique ID generated to couple the trigger and the collapsible body.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Disastrous experience. The trigger has no role and no state. It reads as a passive text heading ending in \"plus\" or \"minus\". When clicked with a mouse, content silently appears below without any programmatic notice or indication of where it is.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Completely locked out. The trigger cannot receive focus via `Tab`, cannot be activated via `Space` or `Enter`, and has no visible focus outline.\n- **Low vision user**: Unless explicitly defined in external CSS, standard browser focus indicators will not appear on a `<div>`. If high-contrast mode (Windows Forced Colors) is active, `<button>` elements receive system-enforced borders and focus indicators, whereas `<div>` elements do not.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Relying solely on `+` and `\u2212` text glyphs without button affordance or interactive cues makes it difficult to discover that the section is expandable.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|:-----------:|----------------|\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Missing `role=\"button\"`, missing `aria-expanded`, unhidden text symbols |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | Unreachable via `Tab`, not activatable via `Enter`/`Space` (WCAG 2.1.1) |\n| **Low vision** | **MEDIUM** | Focus visibility dependent on custom CSS; lacks native button focus outlines |\n| **Cognitive** | **MEDIUM** | Ambiguous affordance; visual glyphs alone convey interaction |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No animation or transition effects present |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No audio or video media present |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **MEDIUM** | Focus ring and trigger styling depend on unverified external CSS |\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Remediation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useId } from 'react';\n\nconst AccessibleExpandable = ({ title, content, headingLevel = 3 }) => {\n  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);\n  const contentId = useId();\n  const buttonId = useId();\n  const HeadingTag = `h${headingLevel}`;\n\n  const toggle = () => {\n    setIsExpanded((prev) => !prev);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"expandable-section\">\n      <HeadingTag className=\"expandable-heading\">\n        <button\n          id={buttonId}\n          type=\"button\"\n          className=\"expandable-trigger\"\n          aria-expanded={isExpanded}\n          aria-controls={contentId}\n          onClick={toggle}\n        >\n          <span className=\"expandable-title\">{title}</span>\n          <span className=\"expandable-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n            {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n          </span>\n        </button>\n      </HeadingTag>\n\n      {isExpanded && (\n        <div\n          id={contentId}\n          role=\"region\"\n          aria-labelledby={buttonId}\n          className=\"expandable-content\"\n        >\n          {content}\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default AccessibleExpandable;\n```\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n**Verdict: REJECT**\n- **Reason**: The component violates foundational Level A requirements under WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) and WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value). Keyboard users are 100% blocked from accessing the content, and screen reader users receive zero role or state information. \n- **Requirement to Upgrade to ACCEPT**: Refactor the trigger to a semantic `<button type=\"button\">`, bind `aria-expanded`, associate with the panel via `aria-controls`/`id`, and hide visual glyphs with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`.\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (unscored)\n- *CSS styling details*: Does the project's design system provide existing accordion styles with a compliant `:focus-visible` indicator (minimum 3:1 contrast ratio and 2px area per WCAG 2.4.13)?\n- *Accordion grouping*: Is this component rendered individually as an isolated disclosure widget, or is it part of an accordion group where only one section should remain open at a time?\n",
